{
 "id": "brahms-lullaby",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Brahms's Lullaby",
 "description": "Johannes Brahms's Wiegenlied of 1868, the lullaby everyone knows, in its old English singing words \"Lullaby and good night\", for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings the tune, the alto rocks along under it in thirds and sixths on the same words, and the tenor and bass sing soft \"oo\" chords from Brahms's own piano part. Two verses, slow and gentle, every line one easy breath: a song to sing for someone.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "lullaby",
  "art song"
 ],
 "style": "free",
 "tempo": 60,
 "key": "Eb",
 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 1,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Arthur Westbrook (1901), after Natalia Macfarren's English (about 1874), from the German: verse 1 the folk verse in Des Knaben Wunderhorn (1808), verse 2 Georg Scherer's (1849)",
  "tune": "Johannes Brahms, Op. 49 No. 4 (1868)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella, on Brahms's piano harmony"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: Westbrook's English was published in 1901 (the Library of Congress, citing Drone's Musical AKAs, lists \"Arthur Westbrook\" among the pen names of the Ditson editor Charles Fonteyn Manney, who died 1951; IMSLP names him as Arthur Edward Westbrook, who died 1952; either way all are dead before 1956), and its opening lines follow Natalia Macfarren's English (Simrock, about 1874; she died 1916); the German verses are folk and Scherer's (Scherer died 1909)",
  "tune": "public domain: Brahms died 1897",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella on Brahms's piano harmony (public domain): the tune as he wrote it, with breaths written in for beginners; the alto on the words in thirds and sixths under it, the tenor and bass on \"oo\" from his left hand's chords",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Johannes Brahms, \"Wiegenlied\", Op. 49 No. 4, in Lieder und Gesänge, Op. 49, first edition (Berlin: N. Simrock, 1868), plate 323, p. 11, in E-flat; the Brahms-Institut Lübeck's scan, IMSLP #23019 (imslp.org/wiki/Special:ReverseLookup/23019), read from its copy at archive.org/details/imslp-lieder-op49-brahms-johannes. The English words and their underlay from \"Cradle Song (Wiegenlied)\", translated by Arthur Westbrook, in Johannes Brahms, Forty Songs, ed. James Huneker (Boston: Oliver Ditson, The Musicians Library, 1903; the song copyright 1901), pp. 63-65, printed in C (University of Toronto copy, archive.org/details/fortysongs00brah), which also checked the notes. The first edition prints one verse with a repeat sign; verse 2 (Scherer's, with Brahms's last lines) and both English verses are read from the Ditson book; the two grace notes left out"
 },
